WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.009
Day 4 of the 31 days of QA + AI series.

00:00:04.559 --> 00:00:07.882
Your AI QA lab: full setup in 30 minutes.

00:00:08.432 --> 00:00:14.025
The lab repo used for the rest of the series: local model, eval tool, Playwright and

00:00:14.025 --> 00:00:15.157
secrets handling.

00:00:16.457 --> 00:00:20.583
Most tutorials on AI applied to testing assume you already have a working

00:00:20.583 --> 00:00:24.540
environment, an API key with credit and time to wrestle with versions.

00:00:24.840 --> 00:00:28.047
In practice, that start is where many people give up.

00:00:28.347 --> 00:00:33.358
This guide solves the starting point: a small, reproducible, free repository you

00:00:33.358 --> 00:00:35.675
will reuse on every day that follows.

00:00:36.975 --> 00:00:39.753
The central decision is to work with a local model.

00:00:40.053 --> 00:00:44.235
It isn't the most capable, but it has three advantages for learning: it costs

00:00:44.235 --> 00:00:48.798
nothing, no data leaves your machine and its errors are frequent, which is ideal for

00:00:48.798 --> 00:00:50.481
practicing how to measure them.

00:00:50.781 --> 00:00:55.707
When you need more quality, switching to a cloud model is one configuration line.

00:00:57.007 --> 00:00:58.704
To follow this guide you need:

00:00:59.254 --> 00:01:03.553
Node.js 22+ and Python 3.10+.

00:01:04.103 --> 00:01:04.779
Git.

00:01:05.329 --> 00:01:08.292
Ollama, to run a local model at no cost.

00:01:08.842 --> 00:01:11.352
Optional: a provider API key.

00:01:12.652 --> 00:01:13.932
This is the setup.

00:01:14.232 --> 00:01:18.449
Pause the video if you want to copy it: the full version is in the written guide.

00:01:19.749 --> 00:01:22.016
And this is the next setup file.

00:01:23.316 --> 00:01:25.582
And this is the next setup file.

00:01:26.882 --> 00:01:27.918
Step 1.

00:01:28.218 --> 00:01:29.800
Versions first.

00:01:30.350 --> 00:01:32.918
Check Node, Python and Git versions.

00:01:33.218 --> 00:01:36.332
If Node is below 22, upgrade first.

00:01:36.882 --> 00:01:40.031
Run the three version commands before installing anything.

00:01:40.331 --> 00:01:45.149
Eval tools and coding agents ask for recent Node versions, and a good share of odd

00:01:45.149 --> 00:01:47.323
install errors come from an old Node.

00:01:47.623 --> 00:01:52.939
If you use a version manager, pin the version in a file such as .nvmrc so anyone

00:01:52.939 --> 00:01:55.265
cloning the repo uses the same one.

00:01:55.565 --> 00:02:00.348
You will need Python later for RAG evals and for measuring performance, so it's

00:02:00.348 --> 00:02:01.558
worth verifying now.

00:02:02.858 --> 00:02:04.057
Step 2.

00:02:04.357 --> 00:02:05.520
A local model.

00:02:06.070 --> 00:02:10.764
Install Ollama, pull llama3.2 and confirm it replies.

00:02:11.064 --> 00:02:14.514
You now have a free model with no data leaving your machine.

00:02:15.064 --> 00:02:22.113
Ollama runs models on your machine and exposes an OpenAI-compatible API on port

00:02:22.113 --> 00:02:22.648
11434.

00:02:22.948 --> 00:02:27.998
That means almost any tool that can talk to a cloud provider can talk to your local

00:02:27.998 --> 00:02:29.580
model by changing the URL.

00:02:29.880 --> 00:02:34.980
A small model is enough to start and runs without a dedicated graphics card, only

00:02:34.980 --> 00:02:35.421
slower.

00:02:35.721 --> 00:02:40.360
Confirm it replies from the terminal before moving on: if that step fails,

00:02:40.360 --> 00:02:43.432
everything else will fail with less clear errors.

00:02:44.732 --> 00:02:45.815
Step 3.

00:02:46.115 --> 00:02:47.453
Repo structure.

00:02:48.003 --> 00:02:53.160
Create the qa-ai-lab repo with evals, tests, data and prompts folders.

00:02:53.710 --> 00:02:55.837
Four folders with one purpose each.

00:02:56.137 --> 00:02:58.659
evals holds evaluation configs.

00:02:58.959 --> 00:03:01.040
tests, the Playwright tests.

00:03:01.340 --> 00:03:03.700
data, datasets and schemas.

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:07.183
prompts, prompt templates as text files.

00:03:07.483 --> 00:03:11.978
Keeping prompts apart from code has a practical reason: they can be versioned,

00:03:11.978 --> 00:03:16.472
compared across versions and reviewed in a pull request like any other change.

00:03:16.772 --> 00:03:20.502
A prompt embedded in a string inside code never gets reviewed.

00:03:21.802 --> 00:03:22.814
Step 4.

00:03:23.114 --> 00:03:24.951
Development dependencies.

00:03:25.501 --> 00:03:30.738
Install promptfoo and Playwright as dev dependencies so versions travel with the

00:03:30.738 --> 00:03:31.065
repo.

00:03:31.615 --> 00:03:36.738
Install promptfoo and Playwright as project dev dependencies, not globally.

00:03:37.038 --> 00:03:41.594
That way versions are recorded in the lockfile and two people with the same repo get

00:03:41.594 --> 00:03:42.462
the same result.

00:03:42.762 --> 00:03:47.414
With tools that change every few weeks, a different global install on each machine

00:03:47.414 --> 00:03:50.137
is a constant source of unexplained differences.

00:03:50.437 --> 00:03:54.802
Install only Chromium for now: what follows doesn't need the other browsers and the

00:03:54.802 --> 00:03:56.117
download is much lighter.

00:03:57.417 --> 00:03:58.546
Step 5.

00:03:58.846 --> 00:04:00.416
Secrets out of the repo.

00:04:00.966 --> 00:04:06.843
Secrets: API keys go in .env, and .env goes in .gitignore before the first commit.

00:04:07.393 --> 00:04:12.095
Even if today you only work with a local model, settle secrets handling before the

00:04:12.095 --> 00:04:12.841
first commit.

00:04:13.141 --> 00:04:17.114
API keys go in a .env file listed in .gitignore.

00:04:17.414 --> 00:04:23.098
Commit a .env.example instead, with variable names and no values, to document what

00:04:23.098 --> 00:04:23.791
is needed.

00:04:24.091 --> 00:04:29.288
A key that reaches a repository, even a private one, must be considered compromised

00:04:29.288 --> 00:04:31.918
and rotated; preventing it costs one line.

00:04:33.218 --> 00:04:34.405
Step 6.

00:04:34.705 --> 00:04:35.869
The smoke eval.

00:04:36.419 --> 00:04:37.745
Run the smoke eval.

00:04:38.045 --> 00:04:42.088
One green case in the table and in the web viewer means the lab is ready.

00:04:42.638 --> 00:04:47.490
The sample file has a prompt with one variable, a local provider and a test with a

00:04:47.490 --> 00:04:48.910
case-insensitive assert.

00:04:49.210 --> 00:04:54.031
Running it verifies the whole chain: the tool installed, the model answering and the

00:04:54.031 --> 00:04:55.064
assert evaluating.

00:04:55.364 --> 00:04:59.453
The web viewer shows each case with its input, output and result.

00:04:59.753 --> 00:05:04.416
Keep it as the first step of any work session: if the smoke eval fails, the problem

00:05:04.416 --> 00:05:07.337
is the environment and not whatever you are testing.

00:05:08.637 --> 00:05:09.743
An example.

00:05:10.293 --> 00:05:14.348
A tester follows the steps on a work laptop with no graphics card.

00:05:14.648 --> 00:05:20.302
Node was on version 18 and the promptfoo install showed warnings; she upgrades to 22

00:05:20.302 --> 00:05:21.581
and they disappear.

00:05:21.881 --> 00:05:26.099
She pulls the local model and the first reply takes several seconds, which is

00:05:26.099 --> 00:05:27.468
expected on that machine.

00:05:27.768 --> 00:05:33.966
She creates the repo, adds .env to .gitignore and runs the smoke eval: one green

00:05:33.966 --> 00:05:34.354
case.

00:05:34.654 --> 00:05:38.348
Then she adds a second test with another country and runs it again.

00:05:38.648 --> 00:05:43.215
In half an hour she has an environment she can clone on another machine and get the

00:05:43.215 --> 00:05:43.876
same result.

00:05:45.176 --> 00:05:46.838
The most common mistakes.

00:05:47.388 --> 00:05:50.920
Installing tools globally and losing control of versions.

00:05:51.471 --> 00:05:55.258
Making the first commit before adding .env to .gitignore.

00:05:55.808 --> 00:05:59.816
Skipping the terminal check of the model and debugging confusing errors later.

00:06:00.366 --> 00:06:03.039
Leaving prompts as strings inside code.

00:06:04.339 --> 00:06:06.582
To know it is done, check this.

00:06:07.132 --> 00:06:10.479
Node 22 or later, Python and Git verified.

00:06:11.029 --> 00:06:13.539
The local model replies from the terminal.

00:06:14.089 --> 00:06:17.343
The repo has the four folders and .env ignored.

00:06:17.893 --> 00:06:21.018
The smoke eval shows two green cases in the viewer.

00:06:22.318 --> 00:06:24.213
And where AI fell short.

00:06:24.763 --> 00:06:28.028
Assistants suggest outdated flags and versions.

00:06:28.328 --> 00:06:31.025
A command you haven't run is a hypothesis.

00:06:32.325 --> 00:06:33.651
That was day 4.

00:06:33.951 --> 00:06:38.925
The written guide, at gastongenaud.com, has the code to copy and the frequently

00:06:38.925 --> 00:06:39.933
asked questions.

00:06:40.483 --> 00:06:45.919
Continue with day 5: Test cases from user stories: the prompt that actually works.
